The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
void batadv_gw_node_free(struct batadv_priv *bat_priv)
{
	struct batadv_gw_node *gw_node;
	struct hlist_node *node_tmp;

	spin_lock_bh(&bat_priv->gw.list_lock);
	hlist_for_each_entry_safe(gw_node, node_tmp,
				  &bat_priv->gw.gateway_list, list) {
		hlist_del_init_rcu(&gw_node->list);
		batadv_gw_node_put(gw_node);
		bat_priv->gw.generation++;
	}
	spin_unlock_bh(&bat_priv->gw.list_lock);
}
```
Reason: The function removes all gateway entries from the list but does not clear the currently selected gateway (`bat_priv->gw.curr_gw`) or drop its reference. This leaves stale state that can break mesh recreation when the module is reinitialized.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
	curr_gw = rcu_replace_pointer(bat_priv->gw.curr_gw, NULL, true);
	batadv_gw_node_put(curr_gw);
```
Reason: Before walking the gateway list, the existing current gateway pointer is atomically replaced with `NULL` using `rcu_replace_pointer`, and its reference is properly dropped with `batadv_gw_node_put`. This ensures no stale reference remains after teardown.

